{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790475680,"nonce":"u9Y4C7l7hLAajrqiSHaXhJj5s+w=","message":"2026-09-27: the wasm benchmark exists (caribou 25a7f81, 10e7c88). It is crates/caribou-interop/benches/interop_wasm.rs plus benches/wasm/WasmBench.hx: the same cells over the same Bench.hx and bench/tally.wren, built for wasm32-wasip1 and run under wasmtime by caribou's own aot::run_module. The program times itself, and a cell whose call doesn't link yet is left blank. Run: cargo bench -p caribou-interop --features llvm --bench interop_wasm -- [--n N] [--runs R].\n\nMac, 200000 iterations, median of 5 runs, ns per call:\n                 Haxe→Haxe   Wren→Wren   Haxe→Wren   Wren→Haxe\nstatic call            3.7         7.2         131\nmethod call            3.4        11.3         156\ngetter                 3.4         9.3       148.6\nsetter                 3.5         8.3         154\nclosure call            20        66.7\nconstruct             27.9       167.4         353\n\nThe hosted bench on the same Mac (ash hybrid, wren_lift tiered) gives Haxe→Wren static 31.3, method 29.7, getter 27.4, setter 30.5, closure 49.2, construct 100.3.\n\nA linked Haxe→Wren call costs about 120 ns more than a Wren→Wren call, so it is slower than the hosted bridge. The time is in WrenLift's export thunk (wlift_aot_raise_stack_top, wlift_aot_enter/exit saving the JIT context, and wren_call_N's generic dispatch by signature), not in caribou's casts. A linked Haxe→plugin static, which has the same cast-and-check shape on caribou's side, measured about 40 ns before 2c1f66c made the after-call check cheap. Wren→Haxe is blank until WrenLift emits undefined externals for a foreign namespace's members. Closure is blank until an Fn cast exists.","files":null}]}